A||N||12||Daleford||Redrilling Fiero front rotors for larger bolt circle?||||5||
Z||000000||Daleford||12-18-2001||09:16 AM||||I want to redrill front rotors for a 4.5 inch bolt circle, but I'm running out of metal at the hub! Actually, I had wanted to do the the Grand Am rotor upgrade at the same time. Is there any larger outer diameter hubs I can use that will fit the Fiero spindle?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||The Fieromaster||12-18-2001||09:23 AM||||If you change the lug pattern you wont be able to do the grand am upgrade because they are 5x100mm circle too!<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif">        <FONT COLOR="red"><B>"Raced That, Wrecked Better"</FONT></B>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif">   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/thefieromastersig.gif">    <BR><B>~In Memory Of Jesse Cesek~ 1980-2000</B><BR><FONT COLOR="blue"><B>AOL IM: Fieromaster</B></FONT><BR><B><FONT COLOR="purple">1985 GT ~ Purple, Black and Yellow Interior(Fastback Converted)</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="maroon">1997 Mitsu Eclipse GSX ~ Maroon w/ Hella Balls!(GSX = AWD, Turbo) DOA on 12/10/2000</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="green">1992 Mitsubishi Eclipse GS ~ soon will be turboed</FONT><BR></B>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Daleford||12-18-2001||11:12 AM||||I could redrill the rotors also.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by The Fieromaster:<BR><B>If you change the lug pattern you wont be able to do the grand am upgrade because they are 5x100mm circle too!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||80's BOY||12-18-2001||12:42 PM||||wwwwwwwwwhy does this not sound safe. is it just me??||||reg||5
Z||000004||Daleford||12-18-2001||12:51 PM||||Done all the time with hotrodders. A lot safer than wheel adapters. <BR>Most of those Mustang II front ends that are being used have redrilled hubs and rotors.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Daleford||12-18-2001||08:32 PM||||Bumpety bump||||reg||5
Z||000006||lowCG||12-19-2001||10:28 PM||||I have little faith in the quality of the Fiero '84-'87 front hubs;they're cast in some sort of pot-metal-like material.<BR>Just because they get all rusty like real metal doesn't mean they're strong like a steel.<BR>How would a good,machined,steel adapter be any worse than messing with those frail little hubs anyways?||||reg||1
Z||000007||88-DOHC||12-19-2001||11:41 PM||||Here is a link to my friends web page that basicly did what you want to do. However, I would only recommend doing this on the 88 hubs, as I don't know how good the pre-88's hubs really are. The 88's are machined steel and very, very strong.<P><A HREF="http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||12-20-2001||11:31 AM||||Id have a reliable machine shop do it if I did. weld all the original holes shut and redrill for new ones in a different spot.Should be same strength as OEM. I dont know if the hubs are tempered or just cast.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Joe Torma||12-20-2001||11:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88-DOHC:<BR><B>...The 88's are machined steel and very, very strong.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The 88 hubs won't fit the earlier suspension...will they?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Alex4mula||12-20-2001||01:54 PM||||Someone should start selling those hub kits. Good idea, specialy for kit cars.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000011||88-DOHC||12-20-2001||08:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B> The 88 hubs won't fit the earlier suspension...will they?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, they don't. Not unless you want to swap your entire suspension with 88 stuff. For the front, I think all you need to do is change out the cross memeber section (i.e. the WHOLE front suspension, steering rack and all).  For the rears, well thats been discussed many times already. <P>A couple other things to consider when doing this. <P>1) The rear hub is actually a smaller diameter then the front (at least on the 88 it is, I can't remember if its the case on previous years). So you can't just redrill them.<P>2) The rear hub assembly is press fitted together on an 88. I don't believe this is the case on prior years, so it makes it somewhat impossible to do much by way of adding a backing plate or what-not as you can't get it apart.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||FTF Engineering||12-20-2001||11:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88-DOHC:<BR><B> A couple other things to consider when doing this. <P>1) The rear hub is actually a smaller diameter then the front (at least on the 88 it is, I can't remember if its the case on previous years).<P>2) The rear hub assembly is press fitted together on an 88. I don't believe this is the case on prior years<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I believe all the rear wheel bearings are the same.<P>Heh... I remember this!!<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B> <A HREF="http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html</A> <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Must've been three years gone by now I collaborated on the engineering behind this project. Good stuff!!<P><B>Still</B> pleasantly surprised that the studs bent over before that front hub cracked!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>-Bruce at FTF Engineering<BR>||||reg||1
